      "body": "\r\n> [!NAV] Navigation\r\n> **Vault**: [[00 - Praxis Framework Overview|Praxis Overview]] > **Section**: [[00 - Overview|Core Concepts]]\r\n> **Pillar**: Knowledge | **Type**: `concept`\r\n\r\n# Comprehensive Study Notes: The Praxis Method Overview\r\n\r\n## Introduction to the Praxis Method\r\n\r\n- The **Praxis Method** is a core component of the Praxis Framework, sitting at the heart of the governance of any project, programme, or portfolio.\r\n\r\n- It comprises two key elements:\r\n\r\n    - **Process Models**: Structured sets of activities that guide the management of the work through its life cycle.\r\n\r\n    - **Documentation Descriptions**: Templates and guidance for the key documents used in P3 management.\r\n\r\n- The individual processes within the method make practical use of the functional procedures, tools, and techniques described elsewhere in the Praxis Framework.\r\n\r\n- The method provides a **life cycle-based approach** to applying these functions and tools in a structured and consistent way.\r\n\r\n\r\n## The Two Process Models\r\n\r\n- Praxis includes two distinct process models to reflect the different life cycles of P3 initiatives.\r\n\r\n### 1. The Project and Programme Process Model\r\n\r\n- The nature of **projects and programmes** means that they have very similar, transient life cycles (a defined start and end).\r\n\r\n- They can therefore be managed using the **same fundamental set of processes**.\r\n\r\n- These processes are simply **adapted** to suit the specific context of the work, and particularly its level of **complexity**.\r\n\r\n### 2. The Portfolio Process Model\r\n\r\n- While **portfolios** share many characteristics with programmes, they have a **different, more cyclical and continuous life cycle**.\r\n\r\n- Portfolios are often semi-permanent or permanent structures aligned with ongoing strategic objectives.\r\n\r\n- For this reason, Praxis provides a separate and distinct process model specifically for the management of portfolios.\r\n\r\n\r\n## The Method as an Integration Hub\r\n\r\n> [!IMPORTANT]\r\n> The processes and documentation in the method provide the central mechanism for the integration of the other main sections of the Praxis Framework (Knowledge, Competence, and Capability Maturity).\r\n\r\n- The diagram illustrates how the Method acts as the central hub, connecting the individual **Functions** (from the Knowledge section) with the desired outcomes of **Competence** and **Capability Maturity**.\r\n\r\n### Relationship with Capability Maturity\r\n\r\n- The adaptation and implementation of consistent methods is a critical factor in developing an organisation's **capability maturity**.\r\n\r\n- The method's processes are the mechanism that integrates individual **functions** (which are assessed for _capability_) into a coherent life cycle, allowing the organisation to achieve overall _maturity_.\r\n\r\n- This is a **two-way relationship**:\r\n\r\n    - The goals and activities of each process provide the basis for defining the **indicators used to measure maturity**.\r\n\r\n    - In return, the maturity model provides attributes and guidance that can be used to direct the **implementation and tailoring of the method**.\r\n\r\n### Relationship with Competence\r\n\r\n- The goals and process activities described in the method provide the structure for defining the **knowledge and performance criteria** in the competency framework. They define what a competent person needs to know and do to execute the method effectively.\r\n\r\n- This is also a **two-way relationship**:\r\n    - In return, the competency definitions provide a **benchmark** for assessing an individual's performance of the processes described in the method.\r\n\r\n\r\n## Flashcard Q&A\r\n\r\n- **Q:** What are the two main components of the Praxis Method?\r\n    - **A:** Process models and descriptions of documentation.\r\n\r\n- **Q:** Why does Praxis have two separate process models?\r\n    - **A:** Because projects and programmes have similar, transient life cycles and can use one model, while portfolios have a different, more cyclical life cycle that requires a separate model.\r\n\r\n- **Q:** What is the primary role of the Method in relation to the other sections of the Praxis Framework?\r\n    - **A:** It acts as the central integration hub. The processes in the method provide the mechanism for integrating individual functions to achieve organisational capability maturity and define the criteria for individual competence.\r\n\r\n- **Q:** How does the Method relate to Capability Maturity?\r\n    - **A:** The method's processes integrate individual functional capabilities to achieve overall organisational maturity. The process goals define the indicators for measuring maturity.\r\n\r\n- **Q:** How does the Method relate to Competence?\r\n    - **A:** The method's process goals and activities provide the structure for the knowledge and performance criteria that define what a competent person needs to do.\r\n\r\n\r\n---\r\n## Navigation & Related Notes\r\n- **Up**: [[00 - Overview|Core Concepts Overview]]\r\n- **Previous**: [[Integrative Management]]\r\n- **Next**: [[Knowledge Management]]\r\n\r\n### Related Concepts & Artefacts\r\n- [[00 - Praxis Framework Overview|Praxis Framework Master MOC]]\r\n- [[Capability Maturity]]\r\n- [[Complexity]]\r\n"
