      "body": "\r\n> [!NAV] Navigation\r\n> **Vault**: [[00 - Praxis Framework Overview|Praxis Overview]] > **Section**: [[00 - Overview|Core Concepts]]\r\n> **Pillar**: Knowledge | **Type**: `concept`\r\n\r\n# Comprehensive Study Notes: Risk Context\r\n\r\n## General Principles of Risk Context\r\n\r\n- **Definition**: Risk context addresses the individual and group attitudes and behaviours that affect the way risk arises and how it is perceived and managed.\r\n\r\n- The context can be viewed as having two primary components: **risk attitude** and **risk appetite**.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 1. Risk Attitude\r\n\r\n- **Definition**: Risk attitude describes an individual or group’s natural, inherent reaction to uncertainty of any type.\r\n\r\n- This reaction is dependent upon their **perception of risk**, which in turn is influenced by a range of factors at both the conscious and unconscious level (e.g., past experiences, personality, culture).\r\n\r\n- The effect of risk attitude is most evident during the **response planning step** in the risk management procedure, as it will heavily influence the way people think a risk should be addressed.\r\n\r\n### The Spectrum of Risk Attitudes\r\n\r\n- Attitudes can be classified in three main ways:\r\n\r\n    1. **Risk Averse**:\r\n\r\n        - As the name suggests, risk-averse people dislike taking risks. They prefer certainty and will often choose a lower but more certain reward over a higher but more uncertain one.\r\n\r\n        - **Positive Application**: When planning work that is risky in a health and safety context (e.g., construction) or when working in a war zone, being risk-averse is an advantage. It means the planner will adopt a very cautious approach to planning risk responses.\r\n\r\n        - **Negative Application**: If a project is developing products for an entrepreneurial tech company in a fast-moving, competitive market, being risk-averse is unlikely to help and could lead to missed opportunities.\r\n\r\n    2. **Risk Seeking**:\r\n\r\n        - Risk-seeking people are comfortable with uncertainty and are often willing to take on higher levels of risk in the pursuit of higher rewards.\r\n\r\n        - This attitude is more appropriate for the entrepreneurial tech company mentioned above.\r\n\r\n    3. **Risk Neutral**:\r\n\r\n        - Risk-neutral people are balanced in their approach. They make decisions based on expected value, without a strong bias towards or against risk.\r\n\r\n        - Perhaps this is the best personal attribute for a P3 manager, as it may be easier for them to consciously move towards aversion or seeking as the specific context of the work demands.\r\n\r\n### The Impact of Risk Attitude on Risk Assessment\r\n\r\n- Risk attitude can have a major impact on the **assessment step** of the risk procedure, particularly when qualitative techniques are being used.\r\n\r\n- Someone who is naturally risk-averse may estimate the probability and impact of a threat as being higher than someone who is naturally risk-seeking.\r\n\r\n- Where major risks need to be assessed accurately, techniques such as the **Delphi method** can help to reconcile the different risk attitudes of the individuals involved and reach a more objective consensus.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 2. Risk Appetite\r\n\r\n- **Definition**: Risk appetite represents the **amount of risk** that an individual or organisation is **prepared to take** in order to achieve their objectives.\r\n\r\n- It is a more conscious and strategic concept than risk attitude.\r\n\r\n- Risk appetite is most evident in the **identification step** of the risk procedure, as it sets the threshold for what is considered an acceptable level of risk to take on.\r\n\r\n> [IMPORTANT]\r\n> The Difference Between Attitude and Appetite\r\n> An organisation's attitude might be generally risk-averse (preferring certainty), but its appetite for a specific, high-priority project might be high (willing to accept significant risk to achieve a strategic goal).\r\n>\r\n> - **Example**: A charity that delivers aid to a war zone must have a **high risk appetite** to even undertake the work. However, its on-the-ground planners will need to have a **risk-averse attitude** to ensure the safety of their teams. Both must reflect the prevailing context of the work.\r\n\r\n- A P3 manager needs to understand the host organisation’s risk appetite as it applies to their work, as well as the different risk attitudes of the team members and stakeholders.\r\n\r\n- During the definition process, functions like solutions development will be heavily influenced by the stakeholders’ risk appetite. A high-risk, high-return solution would be acceptable to risk-seeking stakeholders but not to those who are risk-averse.\r\n\r\n\r\n## Risk Context in Projects, Programmes, and Portfolios\r\n\r\n### Projects\r\n\r\n- A very obvious difference between small projects and large portfolios is the number of people involved. A project is very dependent on the specific attitudes of its **manager and sponsor**.\r\n\r\n- Therefore, the manager and sponsor of a project must be very self-aware and consciously control their natural instincts to ensure their risk attitude matches the needs of the project.\r\n\r\n- **Client-Contractor Context**: Where a project is being delivered by a contractor for a client, there may be different risk appetites. The contractor may be risk-averse to protect their profit margin, whereas the client may be risk-seeking if there are opportunities to increase the value of the project’s output. This can lead to an ethical dimension, where a project manager must balance the interests of their own company (the contractor) with the interests of the client.\r\n\r\n### Programmes and Portfolios\r\n\r\n- At the complex end of the spectrum, the management team must work to manage the myriad different risk attitudes of a large and diverse group of stakeholders to match the needs of the programme or portfolio.\r\n\r\n- Programme and portfolio management teams must ensure that the acceptable level of risk for the work _as a whole_ is reflected in the risk management of the individual component projects and programmes.\r\n\r\n- This **does not** mean that every project or programme will need to have the same risk appetite.\r\n\r\n- Instead, the projects and programmes must be categorised, prioritised, and **balanced** with the overall acceptable level of risk for the portfolio in mind. A portfolio might deliberately contain a mix of low-risk and high-risk initiatives to achieve a balanced risk profile.\r\n\r\n\r\n## Flashcard Q&A\r\n\r\n- **Q:** What are the two main components of the risk context?\r\n    - **A:** Risk attitude and risk appetite.\r\n\r\n- **Q:** What is the difference between risk attitude and risk appetite?\r\n    - **A:** **Risk attitude** is an individual or group’s natural, inherent reaction to uncertainty. **Risk appetite** is the amount of risk an organisation is consciously prepared to take to achieve its objectives.\r\n\r\n- **Q:** Name the three classifications of risk attitude.\r\n    - **A:** Risk averse, risk neutral, and risk seeking.\r\n\r\n- **Q:** How can differing risk attitudes negatively impact the risk assessment step?\r\n    - **A:** A risk-averse person may assess the probability and impact of a threat as higher than a risk-seeking person, leading to inconsistent and subjective assessments.\r\n\r\n- **Q:** Does every project in a portfolio need to have the same risk appetite as the overall portfolio?\r\n\r\n    - **A:** No. The portfolio must be balanced with the overall risk appetite in mind, but it can contain a mix of high-risk and low-risk projects to achieve this balance.\r\n\r\n\r\n---\r\n## Navigation & Related Notes\r\n- **Up**: [[00 - Overview|Core Concepts Overview]]\r\n- **Previous**: [[Process]]\r\n- **Next**: [[Setting]]\r\n\r\n### Related Concepts & Artefacts\r\n- [[00 - Praxis Framework Overview|Praxis Framework Master MOC]]\r\n- [[Capability Maturity]]\r\n- [[Complexity]]\r\n"
