    "02. Governance & Framework/Sponsor A Project Or Programme": {
      "id": "02. Governance & Framework/Sponsor A Project Or Programme",
      "base_name": "Sponsor A Project Or Programme",
      "rel_path": "02. Governance & Framework/Sponsor A Project Or Programme.md",
      "folder": "02. Governance & Framework",
      "title": "The Discipline of Executive Sponsorship",
      "pillar": "Knowledge",
      "section": "Governance & Framework",
      "subarea": "Management",
      "type": "concept",
      "summary": "> [!NAV] Navigation\r\n> **Vault**:  > **Section**: \r\n> **Pillar**: Knowledge | **Type**: `concept`\r\n\r\n The Discipline of Executive Sponsorship\r\n\r\n Intr",
      "body": "\r\n> [!NAV] Navigation\r\n> **Vault**: [[00 - Praxis Framework Overview|Praxis Overview]] > **Section**: [[00 - Overview|Governance & Framework]]\r\n> **Pillar**: Knowledge | **Type**: `concept`\r\n\r\n# The Discipline of Executive Sponsorship\r\n\r\n## Introduction\r\n\r\nAuthority is not a privilege to be enjoyed; it is a burden to be borne. To sponsor a project or programme is to accept the ultimate responsibility for its outcome. It is the act of mediating between the abstract potential of strategy and the concrete reality of execution.\r\n\r\nA project without a competent sponsor is a hierarchy without a head. It descends into confusion, resentment, and waste. The sponsor provides the moral and structural architecture within which the team can operate. This is not merely a title; it is an active, demanding engagement with reality.\r\n\r\n## The Locus of Accountability\r\n\r\nThe sponsor is the single point of accountability. This is the primary axiom of project governance.\r\n\r\nIf the project fails, the failure belongs to the sponsor. The project manager delivers the work; the sponsor ensures the work is worth delivering. One cannot delegate accountability. To attempt to do so is an act of cowardice that destabilizes the entire structure.\r\n\r\nThe sponsor must own the business case. They must articulate, with absolute precision, why the resources are being expended and what value will be returned. Vague intentions pave the road to operational hell.\r\n\r\n## The articulation of Vision\r\n\r\nYou cannot hit a target you do not define. The sponsor’s duty is to speak the truth about the project's purpose.\r\n\r\nThis vision must be communicated clearly to the stakeholders and the delivery team. It serves as the orienting principle when chaos inevitably arises. When the team is lost in the weeds of technical complexity, the sponsor points to the horizon.\r\n\r\nIf the sponsor cannot explain the \"why\" in plain language, the project should not exist. Silence and ambiguity in leadership breed anxiety and paralysis in the ranks.\r\n\r\n## The Shield and the Sword\r\n\r\nThe organization is often hostile to change. The sponsor acts as a shield for the project team against organizational entropy and bureaucratic inertia.\r\n\r\nThe team requires resources—time, money, and talent. The sponsor fights to secure these resources. They protect the team from distracting requests and political interference.\r\n\r\nSimultaneously, the sponsor acts as a sword. They cut through deadlocks. They make the difficult decisions that the project manager lacks the authority to make. They remove obstacles that threaten to derail the timeline.\r\n\r\n## The Relationship with the Project Manager\r\n\r\nThe relationship between sponsor and project manager is one of trust and candor. It is a hierarchy of competence.\r\n\r\nThe sponsor empowers the project manager to act. They do not micromanage; that is a failure of trust. However, they do not abdicate; that is a failure of duty.\r\n\r\nThe sponsor must demand the truth—bad news must travel faster than good news. If the project manager fears to speak the truth, the project is already dead. The sponsor creates an environment where reality is faced head-on, not hidden behind pleasant lies.\r\n\r\n## The Courage to Cancel\r\n\r\nPersistence is a virtue, but blind obstinacy is a sin.\r\n\r\nThere comes a time in some programmes where the correct action is cessation. The market shifts, the technology fails, or the value proposition evaporates.\r\n\r\nThe sponsor must possess the courage to kill a failing project. This requires humility. It requires admitting that the initial hypothesis was wrong. Continuing a doomed endeavor to save face is a betrayal of the organization and a squandering of human potential.\r\n\r\n## Closing Reflection\r\n\r\nSponsorship is the imposition of order upon the chaos of potential. It requires vision, articulation, and the strength to withstand the pressure of decision. It is not a passive role. It is the voluntary acceptance of weight. It is only through this disciplined acceptance of responsibility that value is created and sustained.\r\n\r\n\r\n---\r\n## Navigation & Related Notes\r\n- **Up**: [[00 - Overview|Governance & Framework Overview]]\r\n- **Previous**: [[Provide Assurance]]\r\n- **Next**: [[Sponsorship]]\r\n\r\n### Related Concepts & Artefacts\r\n- [[00 - Praxis Framework Overview|Praxis Framework Master MOC]]\r\n- [[Capability Maturity]]\r\n- [[Complexity]]\r\n"
    },
