      "summary": "> [!NAV] Navigation\r\n> **Vault**: \r\n> **Pillar**: Competence | **Section**: `Competencies & Skills`\r\n\r\n Competencies & Skills\r\n\r\n> [!ABSTRACT] Section",
      "body": "\r\n> [!NAV] Navigation\r\n> **Vault**: [[00 - Praxis Framework Overview|Praxis Overview]]\r\n> **Pillar**: Competence | **Section**: `Competencies & Skills`\r\n\r\n# Competencies & Skills\r\n\r\n> [!ABSTRACT] Section Overview\r\n> Individual performance and knowledge criteria across process, delivery, and interpersonal domains.\r\n\r\n## Notes in this Section (16)\r\n\r\n| Note | Type | Description |\r\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\r\n| [[Capability]] | `Competency` | - **Definition**: Capability, in the Praxis Framework, refers to an organisation's ability to perform a specific P3 mana |\r\n| [[Competence]] | `Competency` | - **Definition**: At its core, competence is the ability of an individual to perform a job or role successfully. |\r\n| [[Delegate]] | `Competency` | Demonstrates the ability to allocate work, responsibility, and authority effectively while retaining overall accountabil |\r\n| [[Delegation]] | `Competency` | - **Definition**: Delegation is the practice of giving a person or group the **authority and responsibility** to perform |\r\n| [[Delivery Competencies]] | `Competency` | - **Definition**: Delivery competencies are the set of competencies that align directly with the **delivery functions**  |\r\n| [[Influence]] | `Competency` | Demonstrates the ability to persuade and align stakeholders and team members to support project objectives without relyi |\r\n| [[Influencing]] | `Competency` | - **Definition**: A P3 manager will often be in a position where the exercise of direct authority is either inappropriat |\r\n| [[Interpersonal Skills]] | `Competency` | - Interpersonal skills are essential for P3 sponsors, managers, and team members to understand the mechanisms by which p |\r\n| [[Interpersonnel Competencies]] | `Competency` | - **Definition**: Interpersonal competencies are one of three groups of competencies in the Praxis Framework, alongside  |\r\n| [[Leadership]] | `Competency` | - **Origin**: The word \"lead\" derives from an ancient term meaning a path, road, or journey. By implication, a **leader* |\r\n| [[Management Competencies]] | `Competency` | - **Definition**: This section of the Praxis Framework includes the competencies that relate directly to the functions d |\r\n| [[Negotiate]] | `Competency` | Demonstrates the ability to reach mutually beneficial agreements with internal and external parties on scope, resources, |\r\n| [[Negotiation]] | `Competency` | - **Definition**: Negotiation is a collective term for various mechanisms that seek to resolve differences between indiv |\r\n| [[Process Competencies]] | `Process` | - **Definition**: Process competencies are one of three groups of competencies in the Praxis Framework, alongside **deli |\r\n| [[Solution Architecture]] | `Concept` | The practice of designing comprehensive technical and operational solutions that fulfill the project scope and specifica |\r\n| [[Teamwork]] | `Competency` | - **Definition**: Teamwork is how a group of people come together to **collaborate and co-operate** in achieving common  | ---\r\n## Cross-Pillar Relationships\r\n- [[00 - Praxis Framework Overview|Return to Master MOC]]\r\n- [[03. Processes/00 - Overview|Life Cycle Processes]]\r\n- [[06. Documentation & Artefacts/00 - Overview|Artefacts & Documentation]]\r\n- [[05. Competencies & Skills/00 - Overview|Competencies & Skills]]\r\n- [[09. Maturity Models/00 - Overview|Capability Maturity]]\r\n"
