    "06. Documentation & Artefacts/Documentation": {
      "id": "06. Documentation & Artefacts/Documentation",
      "base_name": "Documentation",
      "rel_path": "06. Documentation & Artefacts/Documentation.md",
      "folder": "06. Documentation & Artefacts",
      "title": "Documentation",
      "pillar": "Method",
      "section": "Documentation & Artefacts",
      "subarea": "Management",
      "type": "artefact",
      "summary": "> [!NAV] Navigation\r\n> **Vault**:  > **Section**: \r\n> **Pillar**: Method | **Type**: `artefact`\r\n\r\n Comprehensive Study Notes: P3 Management Documenta",
      "body": "\r\n> [!NAV] Navigation\r\n> **Vault**: [[00 - Praxis Framework Overview|Praxis Overview]] > **Section**: [[00 - Overview|Documentation & Artefacts]]\r\n> **Pillar**: Method | **Type**: `artefact`\r\n\r\n# Comprehensive Study Notes: P3 Management Documentation\r\n\r\n## Introduction to Documentation Categories\r\n\r\n- In the Praxis Framework, P3 management documents are organised into three main categories. This structure helps to clarify the purpose and content of each type of documentation.\r\n\r\n\r\n## The Three Categories of Documents\r\n\r\n### 1. Governance Documents\r\n\r\n- **Purpose**: These documents set out the **policies, standards, and guidelines** for the _management_ of the work. They are the \"rulebooks\" that define _how_ the project, programme, or portfolio will be governed and controlled.\r\n\r\n- **Source**: Some of these documents may be specialist documents provided by the host organisation, a client, or a regulatory body.\r\n\r\n- **Praxis Focus**: The Praxis Framework specifically deals with the **management plans** (e.g., risk management plan, scope management plan, finance management plan) that detail how each element of P3 management will be performed.\r\n\r\n### 2. Scope Documents\r\n\r\n- **Purpose**: These documents describe the **objectives** of the work.\r\n\r\n- **Content**: They define the outputs, outcomes, and benefits that the project, programme, or portfolio is intended to deliver.\r\n\r\n- **Examples**: Specification, Blueprint, Benefits Map, Product Breakdown Structure.\r\n\r\n### 3. Delivery Documents\r\n\r\n- **Purpose**: This is the largest and most diverse group of documents. They describe the specifics of _performing_ the work and support the day-to-day management processes and procedures.\r\n\r\n- **Content**: They describe **what** needs to be done, **when** it will be done, and by **whom**.\r\n\r\n- **Examples**: Schedules, Risk Register, Change Log, Progress Reports, Work Packages.\r\n\r\n\r\n## How Praxis Organises Documentation\r\n\r\n- As well as being listed in a dedicated \"Documentation\" section, all documents in the Praxis Framework are **cross-referenced** to provide a fully integrated view.\r\n\r\n- They are aligned with both the topics from the **Knowledge** section and the processes from the **Method** section.\r\n\r\n> [!NOTE]\r\n> This cross-referencing is a key feature that helps users understand the context of each document. It shows both the functional area a document belongs to and the point in the life cycle where it is created or used.\r\n>\r\n> **Example**:\r\n>\r\n> - The **Risk Management Plan** is a governance document.\r\n>\r\n> - It appears in the resource page for the **Risk Management function** (its Knowledge area).\r\n>\r\n> - It also appears in the resource page for the **Definition Process** (the Method process where it is first created in detail).\r\n\r\n\r\n## Flashcard Q&A\r\n\r\n- **Q:** What are the three main categories of documents in the Praxis Framework?\r\n    - **A:** Governance, Scope, and Delivery.\r\n\r\n- **Q:** What is the primary purpose of a Governance document?\r\n    - **A:** To set out the policies, standards, and guidelines for _how_ the work will be managed (the \"rulebook\").\r\n\r\n- **Q-:** What is the purpose of a Scope document?\r\n    - **A:** To describe the objectives of the work in terms of its outputs, outcomes, and benefits.\r\n\r\n- **Q:** Which category of documents is the largest and most diverse?\r\n    - **A:** Delivery documents.\r\n\r\n- **Q:** How are documents cross-referenced in the Praxis Framework?\r\n    - **A:** They are aligned with both the relevant topic from the Knowledge section (the function) and the relevant process from the Method section (the life cycle phase).\r\n\r\n\r\n---\r\n## Navigation & Related Notes\r\n- **Up**: [[00 - Overview|Documentation & Artefacts Overview]]\r\n- **Previous**: [[Delivery Plan]]\r\n- **Next**: [[Finance Management Plan]]\r\n\r\n### Related Concepts & Artefacts\r\n- [[00 - Praxis Framework Overview|Praxis Framework Master MOC]]\r\n- [[Capability Maturity]]\r\n- [[Complexity]]\r\n"
    },
